# appliances/gb10/ida-llm/.env.example
# Copy to .env on the box, fill in real values, NEVER commit .env.
#
#   cp .env.example .env && chmod 600 .env
#
# ── HOW SECRETS GET HERE, AND HOW THEY GET RE-SEEDED ─────────────────────────
# There is no Vault and no External Secrets Operator on this box. In the cluster
# these values arrive as an ESO-materialised Secret from
# `secret/tenants/ida-llm/<env>/app`; here they are entries in this file,
# sourced BY HAND from that same Vault path so Vault stays the system of record.
#
# Re-seed (from a workstation that can reach Vault, NOT from the box -- the box
# holds no Vault credential and should not):
#
#   # 1. read on the workstation; `-field` prints ONE value and no metadata
#   vault kv get -field=OPENAI_API_KEY secret/tenants/ida-llm/prod/app
#
#   # 2. write it into .env on the box WITHOUT it reaching a shell history,
#   #    a log, or this terminal's scrollback:
#   vault kv get -field=OPENAI_API_KEY secret/tenants/ida-llm/prod/app \
#     | ssh uamis@100.103.27.65 'umask 077; cat > /opt/ida-llm/secrets/openai.key'
#
# The rules that keep that honest:
#   * NEVER `echo` a value, and never put one on a command line (it lands in
#     `ps`, in bash history, and in any command log).
#   * `umask 077` before writing, so the file is never world-readable even
#     momentarily.
#   * Rotating a key is: change it in Vault, re-run the pipe, then
#     `systemctl restart ida-llm` (the containers read env at START, so a
#     changed .env does NOT reach a running container -- this is the same
#     env-injection trap the chart solves with Reloader annotations; here the
#     restart IS the reloader).
#
# ⚠ THIS FILE IS AN ALLOWLIST'S INPUT, NOT ITS DEFINITION. docker-compose.yml
# names each variable it passes through explicitly. A value present here but not
# named there does NOT reach any container. If you add a variable, add it to
# compose too -- and note that the compose file uses `${VAR:?...}` for the
# required ones, so a missing value fails `docker compose config` loudly rather
# than starting a mis-configured container.

# ==== Database ====

# Password for this stack's OWN Postgres 17 (container `ida-llm-db`). This is
# NOT the box's existing `gb10-postgres` (16.15, LiteLLM's keys/teams/spend) and
# must not be set to the same value -- a shared password across two databases
# with different blast radii buys nothing and costs containment.
# Generate with: openssl rand -base64 32
IDA_DB_PASSWORD=

# ==== Provider keys (Vault: secret/tenants/ida-llm/prod/app) ====

# The LiteLLM VIRTUAL KEY this stack authenticates with -- the value of
# `LOCAL_LLM_API_KEY` in models.yaml's `qwen-local` entry.
#
# ⚠ MINT A DEDICATED KEY ON ITS OWN TEAM. Do NOT reuse LITELLM_MASTER_KEY and do
# NOT reuse a student/faculty key. A dedicated key is the whole reason this stack
# goes through LiteLLM rather than straight to Ollama: it makes ida-llm's model
# usage metered, attributed and separately rate-limitable, and it can be revoked
# without touching a single student. See README § "Minting the LiteLLM key".
LOCAL_LLM_API_KEY=

# Bearer tokens that authorise `POST /runs`. REQUIRED: this stack is prod-shaped
# and runs with IDA_AUTH_MODE=token, and the api refuses to start with the mode
# armed and no tokens -- deliberately, so a half-configured stack fails its
# healthcheck instead of serving an unguarded endpoint that spends money.
# Comma-separated. Generate each with: openssl rand -hex 32
IDA_AUTH_TOKENS=

# OpenAI key behind the `luna` model AND the judge (IDA_JUDGE_MODEL_KEY=luna).
# ⚠ $25 HARD CAP, enforced OpenAI-side, scoped to that model. It exists so the
# real provider path can be exercised end to end; it is a backstop against a
# mistake, not an allowance to spend. Leave BLANK to run with no hosted
# provider at all -- selecting `luna` then fails loudly on the missing key,
# which is the correct failure, and subjective samples stay unscored.
OPENAI_API_KEY=

# Anthropic key behind every `claude-*` entry. ⚠ NO SPEND CAP. The only thing
# holding these out of an unattended sweep is their `testing-only` tag. Blank is
# a safe default.
ANTHROPIC_API_KEY=

# ==== Networking (not secret) ====

# Where the frontend is published. Default in compose is 127.0.0.1 (loopback
# only). Set to the box's tailnet address to reach it from the tailnet:
#   IDA_FRONTEND_BIND=100.103.27.65
# ⚠ Do NOT set this to 0.0.0.0. The box's only intended access route is the
# tailnet; 0.0.0.0 would also publish on any future campus LAN interface.
IDA_FRONTEND_BIND=127.0.0.1

# Host ports. Defaults are chosen around what this box ALREADY publishes --
# 3000 grafana, 4000 litellm, 9090 prometheus, 9093 alertmanager, 9100
# node-exporter, 9835 nvidia-gpu-exporter, 8888 jupyter, 11434 ollama,
# 8188 and 11000 (host processes). Verified with `ss -ltn`, 2026-11-12.
# Re-check before changing one.
IDA_FRONTEND_PORT=3100
IDA_API_PORT=8001
IDA_DB_PORT=5433

# ==== Reconciler (not secret) ====

# The git ref the reconciler tracks. A TAG or a pinned SHA is safer than a
# branch for a stack with no staging environment in front of it -- see
# README § "Why not bare main".
IDA_GIT_REF=main

# Where the reconciler keeps its own clean clone of UA-MIS/ida-llm. It must NOT
# be /opt/gb10-appliance-repo: that tree is hand-edited and carries the live
# LiteLLM config. See reconcile.sh's header.
IDA_REPO_DIR=/opt/ida-llm-repo

# build | registry
#   build    -- build arm64 images on this box from the reconciled commit.
#               Required today: every promoted Harbor image is amd64-only and
#               this box is aarch64 with no binfmt/QEMU, so a pulled image
#               cannot execute ("exec format error", verified 2026-11-12).
#   registry -- pull pre-built images. Needs (a) arm64 images to exist and
#               (b) a Harbor robot credential for the `ida-llm` project, which
#               this box does not currently have (anonymous pull: 401).
IDA_IMAGE_SOURCE=build
